@*
	The (i) beside a control: a one-line label stays visible, and this reveals the longer "what it does and
	why you'd change it".

	A button, not a hover title: a phone has no hover, and a keyboard reaches a button. The open panel is a
	small floating card on a desktop; under 560px it becomes a fixed sheet pinned above the bottom edge of the
	screen, because an absolutely positioned card anchored to an icon near the screen edge is a card half off
	the screen. Both are plain CSS — no script, so nothing a CSP could object to. Escape closes it, clicking
	the icon again closes it, and the sheet carries its own close button for thumbs.

	AND CLICKING ANYWHERE ELSE CLOSES IT, which is the thing it was missing. The panel is not in the flow —
	that is what lets it be wider than the (i) and land where it can be read — so it paints over whatever
	follows, and on a phone the sheet covers the bottom of the screen outright. A reader who has finished with
	it reaches for the page, not for a Close button, and until now that press did nothing: the panel stayed
	over the field they were reaching for. The scrim below is that press. It is a plain element, not script,
	so the no-JS promise above still holds; it costs the first click after reading, which is the ordinary
	bargain of every dismissable overlay and much cheaper than a panel that will not go away.
*@

<span class="info" @onkeydown="OnKeyDown">
	<button type="button"
			class="info-icon"
			aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
			aria-label="@($"More about {Label}")"
			title="@($"More about {Label}")"
			@onclick="Toggle">i</button>

	@if (_open)
	{
		@*
			Behind the panel and in front of everything else. A span rather than a button, and aria-hidden,
			because it is not a control anybody should meet: Escape and the panel's own Close button are the
			accessible ways out, and a screen reader announcing an unnamed button between the note and the page
			is noise.

			preventDefault is load-bearing, not tidiness. Nearly every (i) sits inside a <label>, and a click on
			ANY descendant of a label is forwarded by the browser to the first labelable element inside it —
			which here is the (i) button itself. Without this, the scrim closed the panel and the forwarded
			click immediately reopened it, so the press looked like it did nothing at all. Cancelling the
			default action cancels the label's activation behaviour, which is what that forwarding is.
		*@
		<span class="info-scrim" aria-hidden="true" @onclick="Close" @onclick:preventDefault="true"></span>

		<span class="info-panel" role="note">
			<span class="info-title">@Label</span>
			<span class="info-body">@ChildContent</span>
			<button type="button" class="info-close" @onclick="Toggle">Close</button>
		</span>
	}
</span>

@code {
	/// <summary>What the icon explains — usually the field's label. Titles the panel and names the icon for a screen reader.</summary>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired]
	public string Label { get; set; } = "";

	/// <summary>The explanation itself: what this does, and why somebody would change it.</summary>
	[Parameter, EditorRequired]
	public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

	private bool _open;

	private void Toggle() => _open = !_open;

	/// <summary>
	///     Shuts the panel, whatever it was doing.
	/// </summary>
	/// <remarks>
	///     Separate from <see cref="Toggle"/> on purpose: the scrim only ever closes, and a scrim wired to the
	///     toggle would reopen the panel on a second stray click.
	/// </remarks>
	private void Close() => _open = false;

	private void OnKeyDown(Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web.KeyboardEventArgs args)
	{
		if (args.Key == "Escape")
			_open = false;
	}
}
